{
  "assessment": "The source packet is a single LaTeX file (arXiv:2509.17085v1) describing universal two-excitation scattering in 2D subwavelength atomic arrays. The extraction focuses on the central quantitative cluster: the S-matrix eigenvalue s(P,E) for two dark spin waves (channel alpha=0) and its universal limits at band maximum and saddle point, plus the associated total cross section sigma_0,tot and branching ratio sigma_0,beta/sigma_0,tot. The source provides explicit equations (Eq. for L, Eq. for s limits, Eq. for sigma_alpha,beta), the definition of the local propagator L, the group velocity scaling v_g ~ sqrt(Delta E), and the branching ratio simplification. The paper reports numerical verification for P=0, d=0.2 lambda_0, sigma+ polarization, with Gamma(p=0) ~ 6 Gamma_0. The extraction encodes the S-matrix eigenvalue limits as two claims (at E_max and E_sadd) sharing the same mechanism and controls. The cross-section scaling laws are summarized in a table (Fig. summary_table) but the specific coefficients are not given in the text; therefore the cross-section claims are qualitative and not encoded as oracle outputs. The source does not provide explicit numeric values for the critical energies E_max and E_sadd, so the control domain for energy is symbolic. The case is authoring-ready because there is at least one numeric control (relative momentum q, though its domain is symbolic in the text), one numeric readout (s(E)), one quantitative mechanism (the L-propagator and s = L(E-i0)/L(E+i0)), and an objective source-grounded response (s -> 1 at E_max, s -> -1 at E_sadd).",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Yidan Wang, Universal Two-Excitation Scattering in Two-Dimensional Subwavelength Atomic Arrays, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/2mvc-8tc4",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/2mvc-8tc4",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2509.17085v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper derives universal two-excitation scattering in 2D subwavelength atomic arrays, focusing on the S-matrix eigenvalue for two dark spin waves and its limits at critical energies (band maximum and saddle point), and the resulting scattering cross sections and branching ratios. The analysis is for a square array with lattice spacing d=0.2 lambda_0 and sigma+ polarization, total momentum P=0.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "local_propagator_L",
      "statement": "The local propagator L(P,omega) is the on-site Green's function, defined as an integral over the two-excitation Brillouin zone of 1/(omega - epsilon^(2)(P,q)). Its imaginary part is proportional to the total density of on-shell atomic spin-wave states, and its real part represents the density of off-shell excitations. Near critical energies, L diverges logarithmically: L ~ -log(|E-E_max|) as E->E_max, and L ~ +/- i log(|E-E_sadd|) as E->E_sadd.",
      "equations": [
        "L(P,omega) = integral_{BZ^(2)} dq 1/(omega - epsilon^(2)(P,q))",
        "L(P,E±i0) ~ -log(|E-E_max|) as E->E_max",
        "L(P,E±i0) ~ ±i log(|E-E_sadd|) as E->E_sadd"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["L", "P", "omega", "q", "epsilon^(2)", "E_max", "E_sadd"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "L_prop_log_prefactor_max",
          "value": -1,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "prefactor of logarithmic divergence of L near band maximum",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 208,
              "line_end": 215,
              "support": "Equation (eqLsaddlelog_final) states L(P,E±i0) ~ -log(|E-E_max|) as E->E_max, with prefactor -1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "L_prop_log_prefactor_sadd",
          "value": 1,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "prefactor of logarithmic divergence of L near saddle point (imaginary part)",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 208,
              "line_end": 215,
              "support": "Equation (eqLsaddlelog_final) states L(P,E±i0) ~ ±i log(|E-E_sadd|) as E->E_sadd, with prefactor ±i."
            }
